// expect: 42
//
// tests/64_large_hex_literal.c0 - a hex literal with the high bit set keeps its exact bit pattern
// Copyright (C) 2026 tavro. Part of coff, GPL-3.0-or-later (see ../LICENSE).
// A hex literal with the high bit set must keep its exact bit pattern,
// not get clamped to LONG_MAX. strtol clamps any digit sequence that does
// not fit a signed long to 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF with ERANGE instead of
// preserving the bits, which is why coff0.c's lexer uses strtoul. That
// parses the same digits as an unsigned magnitude, and the cast back to
// long just reinterprets the bits. A packed 8x8 font glyph bitmap such as
// 0xF88484F88484F800 is the kind of value that needs it. The c0-written
// lexers accumulate with `v = v*16 + digit`, which wraps naturally in the
// register, and they get run over this file too, since it is part of the
// same differential corpus as every other test.
//
// Checked with real arithmetic, not just "did it compile". The hex
// literal must equal an independently expressed value built from
// decimal plus unary minus, at a magnitude safely under the boundary
// either parsing function would hit, so the comparison does not depend
// on the same code path being right on both sides.
int main() {
    int hex_lit = 0xF88484F88484F800;
    int expected = 0 - 539159852467554304;
    if (hex_lit == expected) {
        return 42;
    }
    return 0;
}
